{"birds": [{"name": "House Finch", "sci": "Haemorhous mexicanus", "n": 34, "first": "8:37 AM", "last": "8:55 AM", "ts": 1793713031, "mins": [517, 518, 518, 518, 519, 519, 519, 520, 520, 521, 521, 522, 523, 523, 524, 524, 525, 525, 526, 526, 527, 527, 528, 528, 529, 529, 530, 530, 532, 532, 533, 535, 535, 535], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Native to the West, house finches were released on Long Island in 1940 as pet-shop “Hollywood finches” and spread across the East. Redder males are favored by females.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113005", "snd": "sounds/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113108", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC622745, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "housefinch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/housefinch.png", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 662.0, "beak": [0.12, 0.13, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "B+AAAAfwAQoP8AHOH/gB7wf8Af8D/gH/B/8A3wf/AP4H/4zgB//fwAP/zwAD//4AAf/+AAD//AAAf/gAAD/8AA/+/AAH4B4AB+APAB6AD4A4AAeA8AAAAMAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Eastern Bluebird", "sci": "Sialia sialis", "n": 16, "first": "8:37 AM", "last": "8:53 AM", "ts": 1793713035, "mins": [517, 518, 518, 520, 521, 521, 522, 522, 523, 524, 524, 526, 528, 533, 533, 533], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Bluebirds nest in tree cavities, and their numbers crashed in the early 1900s when starlings and house sparrows took the holes. Volunteer nest-box trails helped them rebound.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113046", "snd": "sounds/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113130", "sndby": "Robin Carter, xeno-canto XC1374, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "bluebird", "cm": 18.0, "img": "cutouts/bluebird.png", "w": 800, "h": 609, "len": 744.0, "beak": [0.69, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 24, "b": "AAPwAAAH/AAAD/4AAA/4AAAf8AAAH/AAAD/wAAB/+AAAf/gDAP/wDgH/8BwD//B4A//h4Af/54AH/94AD/98AB//4AA/P4AAPH0AAHPhAABvgAAA3gAAAPgAAABwAAAA"}}, {"name": "Northern Cardinal", "sci": "Cardinalis cardinalis", "n": 3, "first": "8:37 AM", "last": "8:48 AM", "ts": 1793713067, "mins": [517, 521, 528], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Female cardinals sing too, which is unusual for a North American songbird. During courtship the male feeds her seeds beak to beak.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cardinalis-cardinalis.mp3?v=1793112978", "snd": "sounds/cardinalis-cardinalis.mp3?v=1793113085", "sndby": "David Tattersley, xeno-canto XC1000248, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "cardinal", "cm": 22.0, "img": "cutouts/cardinal.png", "w": 516, "h": 348, "len": 530.0, "beak": [0.06, 0.28, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 22, "b": "AYAAAAeAAAAPgAAAH4AAAB/AAAA/4AAAf/gAAB/+AAAf/4AAH//AAA//8AAP//gAB//8AAf//AAD//+AAf//wEB/v/jwf3v+///+/z//vn945gA+MAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "White-throated Sparrow", "sci": "Zonotrichia albicollis", "n": 2, "first": "8:39 AM", "last": "8:47 AM", "ts": 1793713159, "mins": [519, 527], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "White-throats come in two color forms, white-striped and tan-striped, and almost always pair with the opposite form. Listen for “Oh-sweet-Canada, Canada, Canada.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113071", "snd": "sounds/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113140", "sndby": "Matt Wistrand, xeno-canto XC339648, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "whitethroated", "cm": 17.0, "img": "cutouts/whitethroated.png", "w": 800, "h": 460, "len": 599.0, "beak": [0.73, 0.14, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 18, "b": "AAB+AAAB/gAAB/8DAB/+BwB//h4B//54f//84P///8AAP/8AAA/8AAAD8AAAA/AAAA8AAAA/AAAAeAAAAeAAAAeAAAAHAAAA"}}, {"name": "Fish Crow", "sci": "Corvus ossifragus", "n": 8, "first": "8:39 AM", "last": "8:50 AM", "ts": 1793713188, "mins": [519, 520, 520, 521, 521, 526, 529, 530], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "It looks just like an American Crow. The giveaway is the voice: a nasal, two-note “uh-uh” instead of a clear “caw.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793112991", "snd": "sounds/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793113105", "sndby": "Mike Nelson, xeno-canto XC130581, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0", "key": "fishcrow", "cm": 38.0, "img": "cutouts/fishcrow.png", "w": 800, "h": 474, "len": 557.0, "beak": [0.27, 0.08, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 19, "b": "AH4AAAD+AAAAPwAAAB+AAAAf4AAAH/AAAB/4AAAf/gAAD/4AAAf/AAAD/4AAAf/AAAD34P8Bs+D///Dg/////v////8AD///AAAP/w=="}}, {"name": "Ruby-crowned Kinglet", "sci": "Corthylio calendula", "n": 1, "first": "8:45 AM", "last": "8:45 AM", "ts": 1793713505, "mins": [525], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its ruby crown stays hidden under olive feathers until the male gets excited, then it flares up. A ruby-crowned kinglet flicks its wings constantly.", "vox": "sounds/voice/corthylio-calendula.mp3?v=1793112988", "snd": "sounds/corthylio-calendula.mp3?v=1793113143", "sndby": "Andrew Spencer, xeno-canto XC104679, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "rubykinglet", "cm": 10.8, "img": "cutouts/rubykinglet.png", "w": 800, "h": 653, "len": 568.0, "beak": [0.09, 0.35, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 26, "b": "AAAAAwAAAAcAAAAGAAAADAAAADgAAAAwB8AAYA/wAOA//ADAH/8BwAf/hwAH/+YAB//8AAP//gAD//+AAf/jAAD/wAAAH4AAAD8AAAB+AAAA8AAAAfAAAA+AAAAeAAAAeAAAAPAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Carolina Wren", "sci": "Thryothorus ludovicianus", "n": 1, "first": "8:46 AM", "last": "8:46 AM", "ts": 1793713560, "mins": [526], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its song sounds like “teakettle, teakettle, teakettle,” and pairs often sing duets. It will nest in odd places: flowerpots, mailboxes, even old boots.", "vox": "sounds/voice/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113060", "snd": "sounds/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113075", "sndby": "Jacob Saucier, xeno-canto XC498646, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "wren", "cm": 13.0, "img": "cutouts/wren.png", "w": 800, "h": 633, "len": 781.0, "beak": [0.85, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 25, "b": "OAAAADgAAAA8AAAAHgB+AB4B/+APA//wB5//AAf//gAH//4AB//+AAf//AAH//wAD//4AAP/+AAB//H/AP/f/wA///8AGf/cAB/7AA//8AAP/4AAP/8AAP9gAAD8AAAAcAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "American Goldfinch", "sci": "Spinus tristis", "n": 12, "first": "8:46 AM", "last": "8:57 AM", "ts": 1793713585, "mins": [526, 526, 529, 529, 